maya: Quick repro and question — onboarding sample app intermittently times out (408) and the backend shows duplicate POSTs when a network change or account switch happens during a streaming /v1/generate. Repro: start stream -> toggle cellular/wifi or switch account -> sometimes we see two POSTs, one with Authorization and one without, then client times out. Seen on Pixel 6/8 and emulators Android 13+

eric: do you know retrofit/okhttp versions and how you wire auth? also are you calling call.cancel() from another thread?

maya: Retrofit 2.9, OkHttp 4.10.0. Kotlin SDK uses a suspend wrapper that calls OkHttp Call.cancel() in a coroutine finally block. Auth header is added in an Interceptor. Auth refresh uses a blocking refresh inside the interceptor currently (yeah, I know...). Logs: second POST arrives with identical body checksum but missing Authorization.

maya: snippet of current interceptor: ```kotlin
class InterceptAuth: Interceptor {
  override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
    val token = TokenStore.get()
    val req = chain.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer $token").build()
    val resp = chain.proceed(req)
    if (resp.code == 401) {
      val fresh = TokenRefresher.refreshBlocking()
      TokenStore.set(fresh)
      return chain.proceed(req.newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer $fresh").build())
    }
    return resp
  }
}
```

jing: two quick things: 1) don't block inside interceptor for refresh, prefer Authenticator so OkHttp can resume correctly; 2) centralize refresh so only one network call happens when multiple requests 401 concurrently. Also set `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` for streaming routes.

eric: +1. There's a known header-propagation edge in older 4.10 clients where connection retry (esp. after TLS session resume or network switch) can result in lost interceptor headers. OkHttp 4.11.1 has fixes. Upgrading often removes the naked retry.

maya: thanks. We observed parity weirdness too: our Java sample injects auth at Call.Factory level (wraps Request before enqueue) while Kotlin sample used an Interceptor. The Java sample didn't show the naked retry, kotlin did. So parity matters.

jing: that makes sense: Call.Factory pre-wrap preserves headers at enqueue time whereas interceptor logic may be skipped or reapplied differently on connection-level retry. For samples, standardize on the Authenticator + single-inflight refresh pattern. Example (Kotlin): ```kotlin
// simplified single-inflight refresh
private val refreshLock = Mutex()
private var refreshDeferred: CompletableDeferred<String>? = null
suspend fun singleRefresh(): String = coroutineScope {
  refreshDeferred?.let { return@coroutineScope it.await() }
  refreshLock.withLock {
    refreshDeferred?.let { return@withLock it.await() }
    val def = CompletableDeferred<String>()
    refreshDeferred = def
    try {
      val token = networkRefresh() // suspend
      def.complete(token)
      token
    } catch (e: Throwable) {
      def.completeExceptionally(e)
      throw e
    } finally {
      refreshDeferred = null
    }
  }
}
```

eric: also ensure your Authenticator skips refresh requests themselves: add a header like `X-Refresh-Request: 1` and return null from authenticate() if present. And for non-repeatable bodies you should avoid silent connection retries.

maya: immediate plan: 1) try OkHttp 4.11.1 bump in sample app, 2) switch to Authenticator + single-inflight refresh, 3) for streaming client set `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` and tune Dispatcher, 4) align Java/Kotlin samples to use same header-injection approach. I'll open a PR with those changes and reproducible steps.

